# 105. One Final Perspective

The important transition from an ordinary neural network to an RNN, and then from an RNN to an LSTM, is not that the calculus fundamentally changes.

The calculus is still:

$$
\boxed{\text{chain rule}}
$$

What changes is the **dependency graph**.

For a feed-forward network:

$$
x\rightarrow h\rightarrow y\rightarrow L.
$$

For an RNN:

$$
h_{t-1},x_t\rightarrow h_t\rightarrow h_{t+1}\rightarrow\cdots
$$

so the graph extends through time.

For an LSTM:

$$
h_{t-1},c_{t-1},x_t
\rightarrow
f_t,i_t,g_t,o_t
\rightarrow
c_t
\rightarrow
h_t.
$$

The extra state

$$
c_t
$$

creates an additional temporal pathway.

Everything else follows from carefully applying the chain rule to that graph.

The practical skill is therefore not memorizing LSTM equations.

It is learning to look at:

$$
\boxed{
\text{variable}
\rightarrow
\text{operation}
\rightarrow
\text{next variable}
}
$$

and ask:

> **If the loss changes by a tiny amount, how does that change flow backward through this operation?**

Once that becomes automatic, the LSTM equations stop being a collection of formulas and become a direct consequence of the computational graph.
